        "answer": {
          "short": "The game is not fair: $P(\\text{win})=\\frac49$ and $P(\\text{lose})=\\frac59$.\n\nAn even total requires two balls of the same parity; losing pairs are more numerous.",
          "worked": "There are five odd-numbered balls and four even-numbered balls. Two numbers of the same parity have an even sum; numbers of opposite parity have an odd sum.\n\nCount the two balls in order, without replacement:\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Winning outcomes}&=5\\times4+4\\times3\\\\\n\\text{Winning outcomes}&=32\\\\\n\\text{Losing outcomes}&=5\\times4+4\\times5\\\\\n\\text{Losing outcomes}&=40\\\\\nP(\\text{win})&=\\frac{32}{72}=\\frac49\\\\\nP(\\text{lose})&=\\frac{40}{72}=\\frac59\n\\end{align*}$$\nEach simultaneous pair is counted twice, so these probabilities also apply to drawing both balls together. Losing is more likely than winning, so the game is not fair.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 102
      }
    ]
